TTools native and compat ioctl tests
===================================

Load ttools (CONFIG_TKERNEL_TTOOLS=m) and run as root with CAP_SYS_ADMIN.
The test creates only its own eventfd and temporary anonymous mappings,
and removes its own ptrace protection before exiting.

Build and run on x86_64 with a multilib compiler and libc:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests/tkernel
  cd tools/testing/selftests/tkernel
  ./ttools_ioctl
  ./ttools_compat.sh

The second invocation requires CONFIG_IA32_EMULATION=y (CONFIG_COMPAT=y).
It skips explicitly if the 32-bit helper or runtime is unavailable. For
other architectures, use CC32 to select a 32-bit compiler, for example
CC32=arm-linux-gnueabihf-gcc on an arm64 host with compat support. Both
helpers can be built statically by adding LDFLAGS=-static.

The same source is compiled for both ABIs using the existing ttools
header. It tests exact eventfd reference counts, invalid descriptors,
input and output faults, an ABI-sized buffer ending at a guard page,
permission checks, and unchanged command dispatch. It also checks the
explicit 64-bit layout that existing compat applications could already
use. It does not replace the general ptrace behavior tests.
